## Item 1: the 1 px alpha seam

### Cause

The cause is one lost row in the GPU read of the cut-out buffer. It is not in the row arithmetic.

- The cut-out renders the view at `width * ss` by `height * ss` (3072 x 3072 at `--ppm 1024
  --ss 3`) into the multisampled default framebuffer (`antialias: true`, src/render/studio.ts:24-27).
- The old code read the whole buffer in one call: `gl.readPixels(0, 0, req.width * ss, req.height *
  ss, gl.RGBA, gl.UNSIGNED_BYTE, buf)` at src/render/page.ts:861 (base 1adac676).
- That one call returned raw row 1364 empty (row 0 is the bottom of the WebGL buffer): the alpha
  sum of row 1364 was 0, against 150 for row 1363 and 150 for row 1365. The probe read the same
  row again after the big read: `pixel1364=255 strip1360..1370=255,255,255,255,255,255,255,255,255,255,255`.
  The drawing buffer is 3072 x 3072 and `gl.SAMPLES` is 4.
- With `FORGE_GL=software` (SwiftShader) the same cut-out had no empty row (row 1364 was 150, and
  the empty-row count was 923 against 924 on the GPU). The row loss is in the GPU path of the big
  read. It is not in `resolveStraight` (src/render/cutout.ts:38-79) and not in the supersample
  arithmetic.
- Output row 569 is `(3071 - 1364) / 3` = 569. Its three sample rows lose one, so its alpha is
  2/3 x 255 = 170 where the rows above and below are 255. That is the seam of the acceptance N1.

Probe files (not committed): /tmp/opencode/seam-probe.mjs, /tmp/opencode/seam-scan.mjs.
The temporary probe code in src/render/page.ts was removed before commit 51fee130.

