### The seam rule in the checker

A row is a seam when at least 8 columns have both neighbours at alpha 255 and the row's mean alpha
over those columns is below 0.9 x 255 (scripts/cutout-check.mjs, in the per-file loop).

The brief suggests "a row below half of the mean of the row above and the row below". That form
was not used. A row of a one-row-wide silhouette fails it without a fault: cloud/front row 1008
has a ratio of 0.49 and is real geometry. The solid-column form separates the two: the four seam
rows give exactly 170 over every solid column, and the thin rows give no solid column at all.

### Fix, GREEN

- `readStrips` in src/render/cutout.ts:9-29 joins the buffer from strips of at most 64 raw rows.
  src/render/page.ts:861-866 reads the cut-out through it, one `gl.readPixels` per strip.
- The DEFERRED work: none.

`GREEN_TEST_COMMAND`
(`./node_modules/.bin/vitest run tests/cutout.test.ts --maxWorkers=1 && node
scripts/cutout-check.mjs out/chibi-sprouts/r1/props`):

```
 Test Files  1 passed (1)
      Tests  6 passed (6)
result ok
```

24 PNG lines `ok` and no `FAIL` in out/chibi-sprouts/r1/props. Each of the seam rows is now 254 of
255. The two new tests are green: one checks that strips cover every row once and in order, the
other keeps the row that one whole-buffer read loses.

